  app, other forms will need it later") turned this into a persistence design, and a parameter is an input the sole
  caller has nothing to pass to. [38;5;153mlogin(username, password)[39m stays as-is; a new [38;5;153msession.js[39m holds the identity. That
  departure is recorded in the spec's Problem section and in the decision record, so it's visible rather than silently
  absorbed.

  [1mThe one thing worth re-reading before you approve[0m

  Global Constraint 1. Everything this design persists is a username someone typed into a text box. The [38;5;153msource: [39m
  [38;5;153m"client-asserted"[39m field and the module header make that explicit, but documentation isn't enforcement — if a future
  form uses this value to decide what someone is allowed to do, the structure won't stop it. That's the risk you're
  accepting by going client-only, and it's listed under Open Risks rather than buried.

  Please read the spec and tell me if you want changes. Once you approve it, the next step is the writing-plans skill to
  turn it into an implementation plan — no code gets written before that.

